WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:127826

1
00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:02.135
We made a lot of progress.

2
00:00:02.135 --> 00:00:05.005
Two features implemented with our spec workflow.

3
00:00:05.505 --> 00:00:09.209
But management just asked, of course, for an MVP.

4
00:00:09.209 --> 00:00:12.612
Have we made enough progress to do an experiment?

5
00:00:12.746 --> 00:00:16.249
Let's do a variation of our standard feature spec prompt.

6
00:00:16.916 --> 00:00:18.385
This time we'll tell it

7
00:00:18.385 --> 00:00:20.320
to implement the rest of the roadmap

8
00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:23.823
and give some guidance about existing feature specs.

9
00:00:24.424 --> 00:00:28.294
In general, you should only implement such a large chunk

10
00:00:28.294 --> 00:00:32.198
if you feel confident in the quality of your constitution and spec.

11
00:00:32.599 --> 00:00:34.901
The better the context you provide your agent,

12
00:00:35.135 --> 00:00:37.604
the more confident you can be in getting a result

13
00:00:37.904 --> 00:00:39.706
aligned with your intentions.

14
00:00:40.707 --> 00:00:44.210
And you should be sure that you can handle review and validation.

15
00:00:45.211 --> 00:00:48.915
But since we've taken this risk now, let's view the MVP

16
00:00:48.915 --> 00:00:53.319
as an extreme test of our constitution and completed feature specs.

17
00:00:53.853 --> 00:00:56.656
If we now get something different from what we wanted,

18
00:00:56.856 --> 00:00:58.958
it means we need to very responsibly

19
00:00:59.659 --> 00:01:02.062
carry out another replanning phase

20
00:01:02.562 --> 00:01:05.031
to eliminate whatever led the agent astray.

21
00:01:05.365 --> 00:01:07.667
The agent asked me some questions.

22
00:01:07.667 --> 00:01:10.570
Again, these are good questions and recommendations.

23
00:01:11.171 --> 00:01:13.139
A lot of spec files start as

24
00:01:13.139 --> 00:01:15.375
a back and forth conversation with an agent.

25
00:01:16.576 --> 00:01:19.779
Finished with the interview and we selected Submit Answers.

26
00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:22.082
Let's write the MVP specs to disk.

27
00:01:24.784 --> 00:01:26.786
It's always good to review the specs.

28
00:01:27.087 --> 00:01:31.791
You'll often notice incorrect assumptions the agent made to fill the gaps.

29
00:01:32.292 --> 00:01:34.260
Let's give a quick look at the plan.

30
00:01:34.427 --> 00:01:37.230
the requirements and the validation.

31
00:01:38.331 --> 00:01:42.335
As always, we like to work in small steps with frequent commits.

32
00:01:43.036 --> 00:01:46.339
Let's save the planning stages feature spec.

33
00:01:47.540 --> 00:01:49.275
We are finished with planning. It's

34
00:01:49.275 --> 00:01:51.311
time for the agent to go do

35
00:01:51.311 --> 00:01:53.012
a big implementation step.

36
00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:55.982
The agent now does a lot of work.

37
00:01:56.583 --> 00:01:59.652
Once it's finished, it's showtime. Let's see the MVP.

38
00:02:00.253 --> 00:02:02.455
We'll go to our scripts to run the application.

39
00:02:05.358 --> 00:02:08.461
As you can see, each section now has much more

40
00:02:08.728 --> 00:02:13.333
driven by sample data queried from the database.

41
00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:17.904
At this point, usually we would validate the code results,

42
00:02:18.404 --> 00:02:20.440
but this is an MVP. Let's

43
00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:22.809
ask the agent to validate the specs.

44
00:02:24.611 --> 00:02:26.613
As it turns out, this was useful.

45
00:02:26.913 --> 00:02:31.217
The agent showed places where the MVP found holes in our planning.

46
00:02:31.684 --> 00:02:35.488
We can share this evaluation with the stakeholders for their MVP review,

47
00:02:36.189 --> 00:02:38.191
then merge or archive the branch.

48
00:02:39.192 --> 00:02:41.494
Our spec-driven workflow succeeded.

49
00:02:42.195 --> 00:02:44.497
After a constitution and two features,

50
00:02:44.497 --> 00:02:48.701
the MVP produced a useful demo, thanks to our guidance.

51
00:02:49.202 --> 00:02:52.906
Of course, not all software projects involve building an MVP.

52
00:02:53.273 --> 00:02:56.276
Many projects are so-called brownfield.

53
00:02:56.476 --> 00:02:59.078
They start from an existing code base.

54
00:02:59.279 --> 00:03:02.182
Next, let's see how to introduce this workflow

55
00:03:02.248 --> 00:03:03.783
to legacy projects.
